README-DVI -- the DVI drivers, and which one to take====================================================TeX writes a .dvi, a page description with no printer in it.  A DVIdriver turns it into one printer's language.  The drivers inCMDS/TEXCMDS are one program with different back ends.Take the one that matches your printer.  `dvitype' needs no printer: itprints the DVI as text.The table---------Measured on 2026-09-27 on SYS/TEX/SAMPLES/story.tex.  "Fonts at" is theresolution each driver asks for.  driver    writes        printer                          fonts at  ------    ------        -------                          --------  dvialw    .dvi_alw      Apple LaserWriter (PostScript)   300 dpi  dvidjp    .dvi_djc      HP DeskJet Plus                  300 dpi  dvieps    .dvi_eps      Epson 9-pin, 240x216             240 dpi  dviimp    .dvi_imp      Imagen, imPRESS                  300 dpi  dvijep    .dvi_jep      HP LaserJet Plus                 300 dpi  dvijet    .dvi_jet      HP 2686A LaserJet                120 dpi  dvilj2    .dvi_lj2      HP LaserJet II                   300 dpi  dvimac    .dvi_mac      Apple ImageWriter, 144 dpi       120 dpi  dvioki    .dvi_oki      Okidata 192, 144 dpi             144 dpi  dvitos    .dvi_tos      Toshiba P-1351, 180x180          166 dpi  dvips     .ps           PostScript (dvipsk 5.495b)       300 dpi  dvitype   standard out  none: text                       --`dvidjp' writes `_djc': it is the DeskJet driver built from theLaserJet one, and kept its suffix.No driver takes an output filename.  Each writes beside the .dvi, andeach writes a log to `<name>.dvi_err'.Fonts-----The sixteen fonts Plain TeX preloads ship at 300 dpi, as .pk inSYS/TEX/FONTS/PK300 and as .gf in SYS/TEX/FONTS.  The 300 dpi driversfind them.  The others warn    Font file [cmr10 [120 dpi]] could not be opened.    ---using nearest neighbor [.../pk300/cmr10.300pk [300 dpi]] instead.and scale the 300 dpi font.  For a true match, render the font at thedriver's resolution with Metafont, which is on this disk.DOC/README-METAFONT has the whole account; for `dvijet', whose 120 dpimatches the `epsonlo' mode, it is    export MFINPUTS=.:/dd/SYS/TEX/MFINPUTS/CM:/dd/SYS/TEX/MFINPUTS    virmf '&cmbase' '\scrollmode; \mode:=epsonlo; \input cmr10; \end'    gftopk cmr10.120gf /dd/tmp/cmr10.120pk    export TEXFONTS=/dd/tmp    export FONTLIST=pk    dvijet story.dviRender every font the document uses.  Pick the mode inMFINPUTS/modes.mf that matches the driver's resolution: CanonCX is 300dpi, epsonlo 120.  SYS/TEX/FONTS/read.me explains the directory layout.`dvips' reads its PostScript prologues from SYS/TEX/DVIPS; `<tex.pro>.[1]' is it reading the prologue and setting page one.dvitype-------    dvitype story.dvi < /nilprints the preamble, the fonts the document asks for by name and size,and every positioning command.  It asks five questions (output level,starting page, maximum pages, resolution, magnification) and takes thedefault for each at end of file, so from a script give it `< /nil';without it, it waits.